From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thierry Laurion <insurgo@riseup.net>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] intel-sec-tools: preserve declared KM PubKeyHashAlg when
 signing

When signing a Key Manifest, cbnt-prov's `km-sign` parses the unsigned
KM, computes a signature and then unconditionally overwrites
`KmPubKeyHashAlg`:

  pkg/intel/metadata/manifest/key/manifest.go:
    m.PubKeyHashAlg = m.KeyAndSignature.Signature.HashAlg

The RSA signing path (`Signature.SetSignature` ->
`SetSignatureByData(..., AlgNull)`) defaults the signature hash algorithm
to SHA-256, so the signed KM always ends up declaring
`PubKeyHashAlg = SHA256` (0x0b) even when `km-gen --pkhashalg=SHA384`
wrote 0x0c into the unsigned KM.

Intel requires SHA-384 for the Boot Guard OEM key digest (BP.KEY) on
11th-generation and later platforms (Meteor Lake included).  coreboot's
CBnT integration already declares SHA-384 for the KM public key hash
(see the companion coreboot patch 0003), only to have it clobbered here.

The OEM public key digest algorithm (`KmPubKeyHashAlg`) is independent of
the KM signature scheme's own hash algorithm, so the correct minimal fix
is to preserve the algorithm declared by the unsigned KM and only fall
back to the signature hash when the manifest did not declare one.

This patch targets the `3rdparty/intel-sec-tools` SUBMODULE source tree
(not the coreboot tree).  Paths are prefixed so it can be applied from
the heads tree with:

  git apply --directory build/x86/coreboot-dasharo_v56 <this patch>

Note: heads' `git apply --directory` does reach into an already-populated
submodule working tree, but on a *fresh* coreboot fetch heads does not
check out coreboot's submodules before applying patches, so this file
must be applied manually (or the submodule initialized first) in that
case.  See the accompanying report for details.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Thierry Laurion <insurgo@riseup.net>
---
 3rdparty/intel-sec-tools/pkg/intel/metadata/manifest/key/manifest.go | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/3rdparty/intel-sec-tools/pkg/intel/metadata/manifest/key/manifest.go b/3rdparty/intel-sec-tools/pkg/intel/metadata/manifest/key/manifest.go
index 594394d..cb9601c 100644
--- a/3rdparty/intel-sec-tools/pkg/intel/metadata/manifest/key/manifest.go
+++ b/3rdparty/intel-sec-tools/pkg/intel/metadata/manifest/key/manifest.go
@@ -53,7 +53,15 @@ func (m *Manifest) SetSignature(
 	if err != nil {
 		return fmt.Errorf("unable to set the signature: %w", err)
 	}
-	m.PubKeyHashAlg = m.KeyAndSignature.Signature.HashAlg
+	// Do not clobber the public key hash algorithm that was declared when
+	// the unsigned KM was generated (e.g. SHA-384 required by 11th-gen and
+	// later platforms).  The KM signature scheme and its hash algorithm are
+	// independent of the OEM public key digest algorithm stored in
+	// KmPubKeyHashAlg.  Only fall back to the signature hash when the
+	// manifest did not declare one.
+	if m.PubKeyHashAlg.IsNull() {
+		m.PubKeyHashAlg = m.KeyAndSignature.Signature.HashAlg
+	}
 
 	return nil
 }
